  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $1$ whole split into quarters.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/a2d50ab7d44607b616d4332abcd114b489462e72843ce90b6405a23364917759.png)\n\n- There are $3$ quarters in each group, with $1$ remaining.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/7bda99628d44b1474fc7416e1a6ceb3e79291e85bbfa337a05b4d778e592c062.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There is $1$ full group.\n\n- There is $1$ quarter left over out of a possible group of $3.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1÷\\frac{3}{4}=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
